1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,863
A message comes on your phone. Your credit card bill is ready.

2
00:00:03,863 --> 00:00:11,055
It shows two numbers. Total amount due, thirty thousand rupees. Minimum amount due, only fifteen hundred.

3
00:00:11,055 --> 00:00:22,368
Salary has not come yet. So you think, I will pay only the minimum, right? No late fee. CIBIL score is safe. Many people think like this.

4
00:00:22,368 --> 00:00:26,915
But this is the real trap. ICICI Bank has put one example on its own website. Look.

5
00:00:26,915 --> 00:00:36,791
One customer's bill was sixty nine thousand, six hundred and eighty eight rupees. On the last date, the customer paid sixty five thousand. That is almost the full bill.

6
00:00:36,791 --> 00:00:43,471
But in the next bill, the bank added four thousand, three hundred and seventeen rupees for interest and GST.

7
00:00:43,471 --> 00:00:49,578
Only four thousand, six hundred and eighty eight was not paid. But the interest was almost the same. How?

8
00:00:49,578 --> 00:00:56,600
By the end of this video, you will understand the full maths behind that small number. In very simple words.

9
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,767
Hello friends, welcome to Be Practical with Kishore.

10
00:00:59,767 --> 00:01:05,062
First, the two numbers. Total amount due means the full money you have to pay the bank.

11
00:01:05,062 --> 00:01:13,486
Minimum amount due is the small part you must pay to not be late. Most banks keep it near five percent of your bill, plus fees and GST.

12
00:01:13,486 --> 00:01:17,574
So for a thirty thousand bill, the minimum is about fifteen hundred.

13
00:01:17,574 --> 00:01:23,796
Paying the minimum does two good things. No late fee. And the bank will not report you as late to CIBIL.

14
00:01:23,796 --> 00:01:28,610
But it does not stop interest. And you lose your interest free period.

15
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:35,469
So remember this. The minimum amount is not a safety net... it is a switch that starts the interest meter.

16
00:01:35,469 --> 00:01:47,624
From the day you use your card till the last date to pay, the bank does not charge any interest. This time is called the interest free period. It simply means, for these days, you pay zero interest.

17
00:01:47,624 --> 00:01:57,478
Let's take an example. On October fifth, ten thousand for a phone. On the twelfth, fifteen thousand for a family function. On the twentieth, five thousand for groceries.

18
00:01:57,478 --> 00:02:03,963
On October twenty fifth, the bill comes. Total, thirty thousand. Last date to pay, November fourteenth.

19
00:02:03,963 --> 00:02:09,981
So the phone money gets forty days interest free. The grocery money gets twenty five days interest free.

20
00:02:09,981 --> 00:02:11,811
But there is one condition.

21
00:02:11,811 --> 00:02:18,493
You must pay the full bill before the last date. Even one rupee less, and the interest free period is gone.

22
00:02:18,493 --> 00:02:25,012
This is in the RBI rules. If you do not pay the full bill, the bank can charge interest from the day you spent.

23
00:02:25,012 --> 00:02:31,142
On November fourteenth, you paid only the minimum, fifteen hundred. Now let's see what the bank does.

24
00:02:31,142 --> 00:02:37,740
Rule number one. Interest does not start from the last date. It starts from the day you spent the money.

25
00:02:37,740 --> 00:02:47,359
On the ten thousand for the phone, interest from October fifth. On the fifteen thousand, from October twelfth. On the five thousand, from October twentieth.

26
00:02:47,359 --> 00:02:55,988
How much interest? In banks like ICICI, three point seven five percent every month. That is forty five percent in a year.

27
00:02:55,988 --> 00:03:00,630
Rule number two. You pay interest even on the money you already paid.

28
00:03:00,630 --> 00:03:08,399
Till November fourteenth, interest on the full thirty thousand. Only after that, on the balance of twenty eight thousand five hundred.

29
00:03:08,399 --> 00:03:12,935
Rule number three. On top of the interest, eighteen percent GST.

30
00:03:12,935 --> 00:03:15,742
On November twenty fifth, the next bill comes.

31
00:03:15,742 --> 00:03:22,356
You paid fifteen hundred. The bank added interest and GST of one thousand, nine hundred and eighty one.

32
00:03:22,356 --> 00:03:32,843
So without spending even one new rupee, your bill went up from thirty thousand to thirty thousand, four hundred and eighty one. You paid money, and still your loan went up.

33
00:03:32,843 --> 00:03:38,440
This is our own maths. But we used the same method ICICI shows on its website.

34
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:51,812
The same thing happened to the ICICI customer we saw at the start. Interest on the full bill till the day of payment. Then no interest free period for new shopping. That is how it became four thousand, three hundred and seventeen.

35
00:03:51,812 --> 00:03:59,121
Rule number four. Say on November fifteenth, you fill petrol with the same card. Will you get an interest free period for that?

36
00:03:59,121 --> 00:04:04,965
No. Till the old bill is fully paid, every new swipe gets interest from that same day.

37
00:04:04,965 --> 00:04:12,918
So from the day you paid only the minimum, your card is not free any more. It has become a loan at forty five percent interest.

